{"type": "result", "session_id": "20261018_160144_95a65d", "exit_code": 0, "text": "## What the early Britannica says about Thomas Sydenham\n\nSource: Britannica entry \"SYDENHAM\" (headword, dictionary page), entry_id `page-0251-69952e7c4a0f:e8`, scanned from page_id `britannica:309f765b1ecc83e0a7f7762e44b4a927a7555443:train:251` (file `page-0251-69952e7c4a0f.jpg`, row 251, block `b17`). The OCR renders the early-print long-s as `f` (e.g. \"phyfician,\" \"Dorfethire\"); the words bearing on the facts below are legible and are confirmed against the matched article, so I did not need to re-check the scan.\n\nBritannica's entry states, in substance:\n\n- Dr Thomas Sydenham was \"an excellent English physician,\" son of William Sydenham of Winford Eagle in Dorfethire (Dorsetshire), born there about 1624.\n- He studied at Magdalen-hall, Oxford, but left when Oxford was garrisoned for King Charles I and went to London, where Dr Thomas Cox, an eminent physician, persuaded him to study physic; after the garrison was surrendered to Parliament he returned to Magdalen-hall, studied medicine, and in 1648 was created bachelor of physic.\n- Soon after he was made a fellow of All-Souls College; he then settled at Westminster, became a doctor of his faculty at Cambridge, grew famous in practice, and was chief physician in London from 1660 to 1670, when gout began to disable him.\n- He died in 1689; his works were highly esteemed; he was famous for a cool regimen in smallpox and for giving the bark after the paroxysm in agues.\n\n## Match to Wikipedia\n\nThe match is **accepted** (decision `accepted`, rerank logit 0.88, margin 1.98 over the next candidate), so per the evidence rules this is evidence that both sources describe the same topic: Wikipedia article \"Thomas Sydenham,\" page_id `564669`, revision_id `1341167433`, URL `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Sydenham`, text checksum `e58eb94008793f51432d8c6dfb42e241589ed3f81907ed47805c9d2e4da839f4`.\n\n## Confirmed by Wikipedia (no contradictions)\n\nAll four fact claims the compare tool checked returned verdict `agrees` with both quote flags true, so they are quoted and confirmed:\n\n1. **Birth ~1624 at Winford/Wynford Eagle, Dorset.** Britannica: \"was the fon of William Sydenham of Winford Eagle in Dorfethire, and was born there about the year 1624.\" Wikipedia: \"Thomas Sydenham was born at Wynford Eagle in Dorset\" (passage 1; his father was a gentleman of property, and his brother was Colonel William Sydenham). Confirmed in substance; note the spelling difference — Britannica \"Winford Eagle\"/\"Dorfethire\" vs. Wikipedia \"Wynford Eagle\"/\"Dorset\" — same place.\n\n2. **Bachelor degree in 1648.** Britannica: \"in 1648 was created bachelor of phyfic.\" Wikipedia: \"He completed his Oxford course in 1648, graduating as bachelor of medicine\" (passage 1). Confirmed.\n\n3. **Fellow of All-Souls soon after 1648.** Britannica: \"Soon after, he was made a fellow of All-Souls college.\" Wikipedia: \"about the same time he was elected a fellow of All Souls College\" (passage 1). Confirmed.\n\n4. **Died 1689.** Britannica: \"He died in 1689.\" Wikipedia: \"10 September 1624 – 29 December 1689\" (passage 1). Confirmed.\n\nAdditional Britannica claims I checked against Wikipedia passages of the same article (page_id `564669`, revision_id `1341167433`), all confirmed in substance:\n\n- Educated at Magdalen Hall, Oxford, with studies interrupted by the Civil War — Wikipedia: \"attended Magdalen Hall, Oxford; after a short period his college studies appear to have been interrupted, and he served for a time as an officer in the Parliamentarian army during the Civil War\" (passage at offset 599).\n- Thomas Cox(e) as an influence — Wikipedia: he \"cites the physician Thomas Coxe as an important influence\" (passage at offset 1147).\n- Settled in London/Westminster — Wikipedia: in 1663 he took the College of Physicians licence \"to practice in Westminster and 6 miles round,\" and \"it is probable that he had been settled in London for some time before that\" (passage at offset 1655).\n- Medical doctorate at Cambridge — Wikipedia: \"in 1676 ... he graduated as MD, not at Oxford, but at Pembroke Hall, Cambridge\" (passage at offset 599).\n- Gout — Wikipedia: his description of gout was \"from the symptoms in his own person\" (passage at offset 13344).\n- Esteem; cooling treatment of smallpox; Peruvian bark for agues — Wikipedia: he \"was held in vague esteem for the success of his cooling (or rather expectant) treatment of smallpox ... and for his advocacy of the use of 'Peruvian bark' in 'quartan agues'\" (passage at offset 4986).\n\n## Contradictions\n\nNone. Every fact the compare tool checked returned `agrees`, and none of the additional Wikipedia passages contradict any Britannica statement.\n\n## Not confirmed by the saved evidence (not contradicted — simply not covered)\n\n- That his **father was named William Sydenham**: Wikipedia's saved passage says his father was \"a gentleman of property\" and names his **brother** Colonel William Sydenham, but does not state the father's name. Unverified by saved evidence.\n- That he was **chief physician in London from 1660 to 1670**: no saved Wikipedia passage contains that title or date range. Unverified by saved evidence.\n- The specific narrative that he left Oxford **because it was garrisoned for Charles I** and returned **after the garrison was delivered to Parliament**: Wikipedia confirms the interruption and Parliamentarian service, but not the garrison detail. Broadly consistent, specific detail unverified.\n\nSo the matched Wikipedia article confirms all of Britannica's checked facts (birth ~1624 at Wynford/Winford Eagle, Dorset; 1648 bachelor degree; All Souls fellowship soon after; death 1689) and corroborates the broader career picture, with no contradictions in the saved evidence; the father's given name, the \"chief physician in London 1660–1670\" title, and the garrison narrative are not covered b